{
  "title": "03 — Explore Capabilities Safely",
  "description": "The request has reached a tool. Slow down and inspect the capability boundary from read-only discovery to approval-gated mutation. Each step gets narrower: dispatch, bounds, structured formats, exact previews, stale checks, hidden-directory rules, workspace navigation, and web reading.",
  "nextTour": "04 — Follow State Back to the Screen",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "src/vault-tools.ts",
      "pattern": "^const VAULT_TOOL_DEFINITIONS",
      "title": "03.01 — The model receives five vault verbs",
      "description": "The advertised surface is `vault_read`, `vault_search`, `vault_list`, `vault_inspect`, and `vault_change`. Their closed argument schemas group related actions behind a few deep capabilities. The model receives neither an Obsidian `App` nor unrestricted filesystem access."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/vault-tools.ts",
      "pattern": "^  async execute\\(",
      "title": "03.02 — One dispatcher names the policy owner",
      "description": "`VaultTools.execute` checks cancellation and sends each advertised name to the method that owns its policy. Reads return results; `vault_change` returns a `PreparedChange`. An unknown name fails at this boundary rather than falling through to arbitrary behavior."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/vault-tools.ts",
      "pattern": "private async read\\(",
      "title": "03.03 — A read chooses one window",
      "description": "A caller may select a Markdown subpath, line bounds, or character pagination, but not combine them. The result carries the whole-file SHA-256, selection details, total size, and truncation state. This gives the model bounded evidence and a source identity for any later change proposal."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/vault-tools.ts",
      "pattern": "private selectReadContent\\(",
      "title": "03.04 — Each selector has explicit semantics",
      "description": "Subpaths resolve through Obsidian's metadata cache, character pages support long single-line Canvas or Bases files, and line ranges remain one-based for the model. Bounds errors are reported instead of silently changing the requested window."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/vault-tools.ts",
      "pattern": "private async search\\(",
      "title": "03.05 — Search is a bounded direct scan",
      "description": "Search walks visible Markdown, Canvas, or Bases files and stops at a capped result count. Markdown filters use Obsidian metadata for tags, properties, links, and tasks; content scans check the Run signal. There is no parallel index and no duplicate Bases formula evaluator."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/vault-tools.ts",
      "pattern": "private async list\\(",
      "title": "03.06 — Listing reveals structure without dumping it",
      "description": "A listing can stay shallow or walk descendants, optionally include file statistics, and always honor a result limit. Visible vault folders use `Vault` objects; hidden self-inspection takes a separately bounded path in the next step."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/vault-tools.ts",
      "pattern": "private async listConfig\\(",
      "title": "03.07 — Hidden listing uses the narrow adapter exception",
      "description": "Obsidian does not index its configuration directory as normal vault content, so Bolovan uses `Vault.adapter` only after proving the path is inside its own plugin directory. Recursive traversal and statistics remain bounded just like visible listing."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/vault-tools.ts",
      "pattern": "private async inspect\\(",
      "title": "03.08 — Inspection turns files into bounded structure",
      "description": "Markdown inspection combines the source hash and statistics with cached properties, headings, blocks, tags, links, embeds, backlinks, unresolved links, and tasks. Canvas and Bases leave this Markdown path and enter the structured-file parser."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/structured-files.ts",
      "pattern": "^export function inspectStructuredFile",
      "title": "03.09 — Canvas and Bases remain source-first",
      "description": "Inspection parses useful bounded summaries from `.canvas` JSON and `.base` YAML without claiming to own the complete format or evaluate Bases formulas. The original text stays authoritative, so unknown keys are not lost through a summary round trip."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/structured-files.ts",
      "pattern": "^export function validateStructuredFile",
      "title": "03.10 — The complete proposal must still parse",
      "description": "Before a Canvas or Bases change reaches approval, the entire proposed result is validated for its file type. Validation checks the structured shape that Bolovan promises while leaving unknown source keys intact. Ordinary file extensions pass through unchanged."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/vault-tools.ts",
      "pattern": "private async prepareChange\\(",
      "title": "03.11 — Mutation begins as a proposal",
      "description": "Create, replace, patch, append, copy, move, archive, and trash all enter `prepareChange`. It validates the requested action, path, expected hash, destination, and complete resulting content before returning a title, exact preview, and `apply` closure. Preparation performs no write."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/vault-tools.ts",
      "pattern": "^function exactPreview\\(",
      "title": "03.12 — The preview shows the exact result",
      "description": "Text proposals display the action, path, and exact full resulting content between clear delimiters. The approval modal receives this message unchanged, so the user approves the concrete file that would exist after the operation rather than a vague intention."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/vault-tools.ts",
      "pattern": "private async commitTextChange\\(",
      "title": "03.13 — Text commit rechecks inside `Vault.process`",
      "description": "After approval, `commitTextChange` compares current content with the approved `before` text inside Obsidian's `Vault.process`. If another writer changed the file, the callback preserves current content and the tool reports that nothing was written."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/vault-tools.ts",
      "pattern": "^async function assertUnchanged\\(",
      "title": "03.14 — File operations recheck the source hash",
      "description": "Copy, move, archive, and trash cannot use a text callback, so their closures read the source again and compare SHA-256 before acting. Destinations are also checked again after approval. The prepared operation is valid only while its source and destination state remain fresh."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/vault-tools.ts",
      "pattern": "private assertReadableConfigPath\\(",
      "title": "03.15 — Self-inspection stops at Bolovan's directory",
      "description": "The readable hidden subtree is exactly `<Vault.configDir>/plugins/bolovan`. Other plugins, Obsidian settings, and unrelated hidden files fail this check. `vault_inspect` remains visible-vault-only, while `vault_change` rejects every configuration-directory source or destination even inside Bolovan."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/workspace-tools.ts",
      "pattern": "^export class WorkspaceTools",
      "title": "03.16 — Workspace context is another bounded capability",
      "description": "The `workspace` tool either reports the active Markdown editor and bounded selection or opens a visible vault file at the user's request. Navigation chooses another leaf rather than replacing Bolovan's chat, preserving the conversation surface while the user moves through notes."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/web-content.ts",
      "pattern": "^export class WebContentReader",
      "title": "03.17 — Web access reads one supplied URL",
      "description": "`web_read` accepts one HTTP(S) URL, fetches through the injected Obsidian request transport, extracts bounded readable text, and treats page content as untrusted source material. It does not search the web or turn page text into instructions."
    },
    {
      "file": "tests/vault-tools.test.ts",
      "pattern": "describe\\(\"VaultTools reads and discovery\"",
      "title": "03.18 — Prove the read side first",
      "description": "These tests drive bounded lines and character pages, heading resolution, listing, metadata filters, cancellation, Markdown inspection, and Canvas/Bases discovery through the public `VaultTools` interface. They establish what evidence a model can obtain before proposing a change."
    },
    {
      "file": "tests/vault-tools.test.ts",
      "pattern": "describe\\(\"VaultTools exact changes\"",
      "title": "03.19 — Then prove the write boundary",
      "description": "The exact-change suite exercises previews, structured validation, stale rejection, native lifecycle operations, and the hidden-directory seal with a synthetic Obsidian app. Once those boundaries are clear, continue to chapter 04 to see how state returns to the screen."
    }
  ]
}
